  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 254 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level low continues to build into the \nregion today, bringing a couple more rounds of showers. The most\nwidespread showers look to be in the afternoon and early \nevening as a broken line pushes down from the north. These will \nagain be convective, and with temperatures aloft continuing to \ncool, a bit of graupel or pea sized hail will be possible out of\nthe strongest showers. Shorter storms will make thunder \nunlikely, though it cannot be completely ruled out. Snow levels \nlook to drop to around 4,000 feet before the precipitation exits\nthis evening, so a couple flurries/snow showers are possible in\nthe highest peaks. While hypothetical snow levels will continue\nto fall tonight, any sufficient moisture capable of producing \nprecipitation will have exited, though a few lingering low \nclouds could produce rime ice. Surface high pressure builds into\nthe region for tomorrow, leading to mostly sunny skies, \nlightening winds and high temperatures in the 50s for most \nplaces. Relatively widespread frost is likely tomorrow morning, \nbut winds look to remain elevated for a longer portion of Friday\nnight, so its extent in the Champlain looks somewhat limited \nand uncertain, though it could be enough for a Frost Advisory to\nbe issued today. High pressure pushes to the east for Sunday \nand return southerly flow will cause temperatures to rise into \nthe 60s. High clouds ahead of Isaias will infiltrate the region \nduring the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The center of the remnants of Isaias will track \nwell to our south Sunday night into Monday morning, but the \nprecipitation shield will extend far north so showers and a \nperiod of stratiform rain are expected away from the \ninternational border. Totals ranging up to a half inch are \ncurrently expected, with the highest values farther south. There\nwill likely be a sharp gradient on the northern end and where \nthis exactly sets up remains uncertain. The rain should move out\nfor most of the day monday though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A low pressure system will track north of our \nforecast area, and a surface cold front drops south across our \narea on Tuesday. This front crossing the area brings our next \nchance for showers to the region. Following this frontal passage\ntemperatures will trend below normal for a couple days.",
